   title: OpenSpatialWorld API
   description: "The solution allows you to use a web app to enter the associated spatial experience and read and modify aspects of the world. 
     The endpoint provides a single discrete world with some aspects of spatial data management. The main purpose is to join, connect and share the spatial world.\n\n\
-    Frames. All coordinates are in meters, right-handed, +Y up. A Spatial's GeoPose anchors its origin: at zero angles +X is east, +Y up and +Z south. All other positions are local to their parent."
+    Frames. All coordinates are in meters, right-handed, +Y up. A Spatial's GeoPose anchors its origin: at zero angles +X is east, +Y up and +Z south. All other positions are local to their parent.\n\n\
+    Media type. Resources are served as application/wow+json, a working name that stays provisional until the group decides it. It is JSON, and its fragment identifiers are JSON Pointers (RFC 6901, section 6). application/json stays available by content negotiation.\n\n\
+    Entity tags. GET /wow/world, GET /wow/spatial/{spatialID} and the node GET return ETag and Last-Modified, and answer If-None-Match with 304. The node PUT and DELETE honor If-Match and answer 412 when it does not match (RFC 9110).\n\n\
+    Cross-origin access. Responses carry Access-Control-Allow-Origin as the world's policy allows, and Access-Control-Expose-Headers: ETag. Preflight responses allow the methods GET, HEAD, POST, PUT and DELETE, and the request headers Authorization, Content-Type, If-None-Match and If-Match."
   version: '0.0.1'
 
